---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const pageTitle = "Shade Variation Explained: Why Dye Lots Don't Always Match";
const pageDescription = "Why fabric from the same order can show slightly different shades, how shade variation is measured, and what tolerance is normal versus a real quality problem.";
const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": pageTitle,
    "description": pageDescription,
    "author": { "@id": "https://sdfltd.com/#org" },
    "publisher": { "@id": "https://sdfltd.com/#org" },
    "url": "https://sdfltd.com/insights/shade-variation-explained/",
    "mainEntityOfPage": "https://sdfltd.com/insights/shade-variation-explained/"
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What grey scale rating should I expect from a good factory?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A rating of 4 or better is standard for most commercial orders. Rating 3 or below is generally considered a quality failure worth rejecting or renegotiating."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can shade variation be completely eliminated?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No — some lot-to-lot variation is a normal part of industrial dyeing. The realistic goal is keeping it inside an agreed, measured tolerance, not eliminating it entirely."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does shade variation mean the factory used a cheaper dye?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Not necessarily. Variation is more often caused by dyeing conditions (temperature, timing, machine load) or by mixing fabric rolls at cutting than by dye quality itself."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Should I inspect for shade variation myself or rely on the factory's QC?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Both. Ask for your factory's shade-band photos as part of pre-shipment inspection, and if you use a third-party QC service, confirm shade consistency across cartons is explicitly on their checklist — it's not always included by default."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} schema={schema}>

  <div class="container" style="padding: 3rem 1.5rem 5rem;">

    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <p style="font-size: 0.72rem; color: #FFFFFF; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase;">
        <a href="/" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">Home</a>
        <span style="margin: 0 0.5rem; color: #333;">›</span>
        <a href="/insights" style="color: #aaa; text-decoration: none;">Insights</a>
        <span style="margin: 0 0.5rem; color: #333;">›</span>
        <span style="color: #FFFFFF;">Shade Variation Explained</span>
      </p>
    </nav>

    <article>

      <header style="max-width: 760px; margin-bottom: 3rem; padding-bottom: 2rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <span style="font-size: 0.62rem; letter-spacing: 0.25em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #e00; display: block; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Production Guide · Dyeing &amp; Color</span>
        <h1 style="font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.8rem); font-weight: 200; color: #fff; line-height: 1.2; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; letter-spacing: 0.01em;">
          Shade Variation Explained: Why Dye Lots Don't Always Match
        </h1>
        <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #aaa; line-height: 1.9; margin: 0;">
          Shade variation is the natural color difference between two dye lots, caused by small differences in temperature, timing, and fabric batch during dyeing. It's normal in industrial production. The real issue is whether it stays inside an agreed tolerance, usually measured on a 1-5 grey scale, and whether cutting was done from mixed fabric rolls, which is a preventable factory error rather than a natural one.
        </p>
      </header>

      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">Why Two Pieces From the Same Order Don't Always Match</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Dye is mixed and applied in batches called dye lots. Even with an identical recipe, each lot can absorb color slightly differently, because dye uptake depends on water temperature, machine load, dyeing time, and the specific greige fabric batch being dyed — none of which are perfectly identical from one lot to the next.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">This is why a factory dyes enough fabric for an entire order in as few lots as possible, and why cutting all pieces of one garment (front, back, sleeve) from the same fabric roll matters more than most buyers realize.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">Small variation between lots is normal and unavoidable at industrial scale. The real question isn't whether variation exists, but whether it stays inside an agreed, measurable tolerance.</p>
      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">How Shade Variation Is Actually Measured</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Factories and buyers use a grey scale rating, a standardized 1-5 scale where 5 means no visible difference and 1 means a strongly visible difference. Most commercial garment orders are accepted at a grey scale rating of 4 or better — a difference detectable only under close side-by-side comparison, not to a casual eye.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">Some brands also specify a Delta E value, a more precise numeric color-difference measurement from a spectrophotometer. A Delta E under roughly 1.0 is considered an excellent match; under 2.0 is generally acceptable for most apparel; above that, most buyers will reject the lot.</p>
      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">The Cutting-Table Mistake That Creates Visible Variation</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Shade variation inside a single garment — a body that's a slightly different shade from its own sleeve — is almost never a dyeing problem. It's a cutting-table problem: panels for one garment were cut from two different fabric rolls or two different dye lots.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">This is preventable with a simple rule: bundle and cut by roll number, not just by color name. A well-run cutting floor tracks which roll each panel came from, and never mixes rolls within the same garment.</p>
      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">Lot-to-Lot Variation vs Within-Lot Variation</h2>
      <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: 2rem; font-size: 0.9rem;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #333;">
            <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; color: #fff; font-weight: 400;">Type</th>
            <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #fff; font-weight: 400;">What It Means</th>
            <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem; color: #fff; font-weight: 400;">Typical Cause</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;">
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; color: #aaa;">Lot-to-lot</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #aaa;">Order shipped in batches shows slight shade drift across cartons</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem; color: #aaa;">Different dye lots used across a large order</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;">
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; color: #aaa;">Within-lot, cross-panel</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #aaa;">One garment's body doesn't match its own sleeve or collar</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem; color: #aaa;">Cutting from mixed fabric rolls — a preventable factory error</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; color: #aaa;">Edge-to-center</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #aaa;">Fabric roll shows lighter/darker shade from edge to center</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem; color: #aaa;">Uneven dye penetration — usually caught at fabric inspection, before cutting</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">The first type is a normal, manageable production reality. The second and third types should be caught before the garment ever reaches you, at fabric inspection or during cutting — if they aren't, that's a quality-control gap worth raising with your factory directly.</p>
      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">What to Ask Your Factory Before Bulk Dyeing</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Ask what grey scale tolerance they work to, and get it in writing alongside your order. Ask whether they dye your full order quantity in one lot or split across multiple lots — for large orders, splitting is often unavoidable, but you should know it's happening.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">For color-critical orders, request that your QC inspection specifically checks shade consistency across cartons, not just within a single sample garment.</p>
      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">Shade Variation FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq-accordion2">
        <div class="faq-item2">
          <button type="button" class="faq-question2" aria-expanded="false">
            What grey scale rating should I expect from a good factory?
            <span class="faq-icon2" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer2">
            <p>A rating of 4 or better is standard for most commercial orders. Rating 3 or below is generally considered a quality failure worth rejecting or renegotiating.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item2">
          <button type="button" class="faq-question2" aria-expanded="false">
            Can shade variation be completely eliminated?
            <span class="faq-icon2" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer2">
            <p>No — some lot-to-lot variation is a normal part of industrial dyeing. The realistic goal is keeping it inside an agreed, measured tolerance, not eliminating it entirely.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item2">
          <button type="button" class="faq-question2" aria-expanded="false">
            Does shade variation mean the factory used a cheaper dye?
            <span class="faq-icon2" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer2">
            <p>Not necessarily. Variation is more often caused by dyeing conditions (temperature, timing, machine load) or by mixing fabric rolls at cutting than by dye quality itself.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item2">
          <button type="button" class="faq-question2" aria-expanded="false">
            Should I inspect for shade variation myself or rely on the factory's QC?
            <span class="faq-icon2" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer2">
            <p>Both. Ask for your factory's shade-band photos as part of pre-shipment inspection, and if you use a third-party QC service, confirm shade consistency across cartons is explicitly on their checklist — it's not always included by default.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div style="text-align: center; padding: 2.5rem; border: 1px solid #1a1a1a; border-top: 0.6px solid #e00; background: #050505; margin-top: 3rem;">
        <p style="color: #fff; font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-weight: 300;">
          Ordering a color-critical run and want shade consistency checked at every stage? SDF Clothing tracks fabric rolls from dye lot through cutting, with documented shade-band checks before shipment.
        </p>
        <a href="/dyeing-service" style="display: inline-block; background: #e00; color: #fff; padding: 0.9rem 2.5rem; text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.15em; text-transform: uppercase; transition: background 0.2s;">
          Discuss Your Color Requirements ›
        </a>
      </div>

      <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #aaa; margin-top: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.8;">
        Curious what happens before dyeing even starts? <a href="/insights/lab-dip-explained" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">Read Lab Dip Explained</a> to understand the color approval process.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #aaa; margin-top: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.8;">
        Need to check fabric weight consistency too? <a href="/insights/fabric-gsm-explained" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">See Fabric GSM Explained</a>.
      </p>


    </article>

  </div>


    <style>
      .faq-item2 { border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a; }
      .faq-item2:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
      .faq-question2 { width: 100%; background: none; border: none; color: #fff; text-align: left; cursor: pointer; padding: 1.1rem 0; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 1rem; font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 400; font-family: inherit; min-height: 44px; transition: color 0.2s; }
      .faq-question2:hover { color: #e00; }
      .faq-icon2 { flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 1.4rem; line-height: 1; color: #e00; transition: transform 0.2s; }
      .faq-icon2--open { transform: rotate(45deg); }
      .faq-answer2 { display: none; padding-bottom: 1.1rem; }
      .faq-answer2--visible { display: block; }
      .faq-answer2 p { color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0; }
    </style>
    <script is:inline>
      document.querySelectorAll('.faq-question2').forEach(function (button) {
        button.addEventListener('click', function () {
          const answer = button.nextElementSibling;
          const icon = button.querySelector('.faq-icon2');
          const isExpanded = button.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
          button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!isExpanded));
          answer.classList.toggle('faq-answer2--visible');
          icon.classList.toggle('faq-icon2--open');
        });
      });
    </script>

</BaseLayout>
